Yemen might have had a coffee monopoly and Mecca might have been the place where it

196
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:33,840
caught on with the masses but before long- the capital of coffee culture was about to emerge

197
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:46,720
in a city called Istanbul. According to legend,

198
00:19:46,720 --> 00:19:53,680
the first Istanbul coffeeshop opened as far back as 1475; the iconic Kiva Han. The truth is it was

199
00:19:53,680 --> 00:19:57,920
probably built at least a little while later, but who cares? It would be part of a movement

200
00:19:57,920 --> 00:20:02,960
that would change the world, and set us on a course leading all the way to the present.

201
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:07,920
Basically, in the past coffee was a drink. That’s it. Something you consume.

202
00:20:07,920 --> 00:20:12,880
But in Istanbul it would become a way of life. By the time we find accounts of Turkish cafes,

203
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:18,480
we read of enormous spaces, bright and open, buzzing with people and energy. The air was

204
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:24,000
rich with the smell of roasting coffee, and loud- echoing with crowds laughing, gossiping,

205
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:28,880
talking politics or playing games like chess or backgammon. Sometimes there would be a band

206
00:20:28,880 --> 00:20:34,560
or musician on stage, or a poet doing a reading. And there were no private tables; everyone shared

207
00:20:34,560 --> 00:20:40,480
a floor together, regardless of rank or status. It turned out coffee didn’t just give energy for

208
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:45,360
fighting or dancing- it was equally effective at sparking conversation,

209
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:51,040
and before long Istanbul’s cafes became known as “street universities,” or “schools of the wise.”

210
00:20:51,040 --> 00:20:56,560
And the entire culture of the empire would shift dramatically. In these cafes artists

211
00:20:56,560 --> 00:21:02,160
would debut new works and find inspiration. New forms of theater and literature would develop,

212
00:21:02,160 --> 00:21:07,200
and the brightest minds in academics could meet and share ideas freely, outside of the

213
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:11,920
structured order of government and university. It was not a coincidence that the rise in

214
00:21:11,920 --> 00:21:17,280
Istanbul’s cafes aligned exactly with the Ottoman Golden Age in the years of Suleiman

215
00:21:17,280 --> 00:21:23,200
the Magnificent, and it was also not a coincidence that in 1538, Suleiman would invade and conquer

216
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:27,600
Yemen, and seize control of al-Mocha. And for the next hundred years it

217
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:31,760
would be the Turks, not the Yemenis, profiting from the coffee monopoly.

218
00:21:31,760 --> 00:21:35,920
Anyway let’s go back to those cafes. At some point, late in the 16th century,

219
00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:41,520
it started dawning on Ottoman rulers- especially those who were not “magnificent”- that centers of

220
00:21:41,520 --> 00:21:47,280
free speech might be dangerous. And we start to see crackdowns and even a few short-lived bans.

221
00:21:47,280 --> 00:21:53,680
But none of it would actually stick until 1633 when Sultan Murad IV- terribly unpopular and

222
00:21:53,680 --> 00:21:58,880
terrified of being overthrown- declared with no warning that visiting a cafe was now a

223
00:21:58,880 --> 00:22:03,680
capital offense. There would be no public meetings, no unauthorized gatherings and

224
00:22:03,680 --> 00:22:08,720
absolutely no coffee whatsoever. But that is not the crazy part.

225
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:13,840
No the crazy part is that Murad IV was so paranoid that he used to get incredibly drunk,

226
00:22:13,840 --> 00:22:19,280
dress up as a beggar, walk through the streets of Istanbul and personally hunt for anyone violating

227
00:22:19,280 --> 00:22:25,520
the new law. Whenever he would catch the smell of coffee, or see any person having a sip, he would

228
00:22:25,520 --> 00:22:31,200
rip off his shawl, announce his true identity and then cut off their head with his own sword.

229
00:22:31,200 --> 00:22:35,280
Thankfully the Sultan would die at the age of 27 from cirrhosis;

230
00:22:35,280 --> 00:22:41,200
all those drunken nights catching up to him quickly, and Istanbul’s café scene would recover.

231
00:22:41,200 --> 00:23:02,560
Let’s take a break for some Turkish coffee. 

232
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:08,080
There are few things on earth like a café in Istanbul. But we are not in Istanbul,

233
00:23:08,080 --> 00:23:13,760
we’re in Bangkok, so we’re making due by visiting the house of a Turkish family, recently converted

234
00:23:13,760 --> 00:23:46,320
into a home-style restaurant. 

235
00:23:46,320 --> 00:23:51,280
The most common legend about Turkish coffee is that its method was developed in Suleiman’s

236
00:23:51,280 --> 00:23:56,080
royal palace. In the past, Turkish coffee had been prepared just like in Yemen,

237
00:23:56,080 --> 00:24:01,680
which itself was adapted almost entirely from Ethiopia. But Suleiman was so fascinated by

238
00:24:01,680 --> 00:24:07,520
this new drink he appointed a coffee master- assisted by a team of 40 roasters, grinders,

239
00:24:07,520 --> 00:24:13,280
and brewers and that led to this. The idea of Turkish coffee is to grind the beans

240
00:24:13,280 --> 00:24:18,320
into a fine powder- much finer than modern coffee grounds. Then to heat it with water

241
00:24:18,320 --> 00:24:24,080
and stir to create a rich and thick froth without burning the beans. And while today

242
00:24:24,080 --> 00:24:29,520
technology has advanced the basic concept is the same- and every cup tells a story

243
00:24:29,520 --> 00:24:46,160
of history. The taste and superstitions we might have found in Istanbul 500 years ago.

244
00:24:46,160 --> 00:24:54,400
 Ok. I think- what

245
00:24:54,400 --> 00:25:11,920
is it? That looks like the Taj Mahal. Business. Ok. It’s a fish again. You’re

246
00:25:11,920 --> 00:25:18,880
just seeing fish. Always good. Fish is always good! It’s abundance. Fish is abundance. Yeah. Is

247
00:25:18,880 --> 00:25:33,360
it a fish? I mean…corn?  It looks like a bat hanging upside down. Well, you’re also looking

248
00:25:33,360 --> 00:25:44,640
from the other angle. It’s right side up to us. Ooh, that’s a bat. In Rorschach tests, I would

249
00:25:44,640 --> 00:26:00,480
fail. It’s just blips for me. A dog! Dog? Look. Nose, ears… Have you ever seen a dog before? Goofy

250
00:26:00,480 --> 00:26:22,480
the Dog. A woman? You see a woman? Adam! Business. You see anything else? I think that’s a cut.

251
00:26:22,480 --> 00:26:26,240
In the heyday of Istanbul’s café scene, with the Turkish economy booming,

252
00:26:26,240 --> 00:26:31,680
Suleiman the Magnificent went on the warpath, capturing Yemen and a whole lot more.

253
00:26:31,680 --> 00:26:37,280
As early as 1521 he’d established a foothold in Europe, taking Belgrade of today’s Serbia,

254
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:42,720
where immediately a coffeeshop would open- the first ever on European soil. And this would repeat

255
00:26:42,720 --> 00:26:48,400
itself over and over as the Ottomans pushed west. The Turkish style of coffee, thick, unfiltered,

256
00:26:48,400 --> 00:26:53,760
the stuff we just had at the last restaurant- well wherever you find something that looks like that,

257
00:26:53,760 --> 00:26:58,640
you’re probably in the old Ottoman territory. Turkish coffee would become the style consumed

258
00:26:58,640 --> 00:27:04,720
to this day in the Balkans, Ukraine, and Greece, all thanks to the conquests of Suleiman.

259
00:27:04,720 --> 00:27:09,760
And the Ottoman expansion is also how the word became standardized everywhere- Yemeni

260
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:16,000
“Gahwah” becoming Turkish “Kahve” which would be the root of coffee and café.

261
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:20,240
Anyway speaking of cafes, the Turks might have introduced the idea of coffeeshops as

262
00:27:20,240 --> 00:27:25,280
places for gathering, but there were still a few major elements missing from what we know today.

263
00:27:25,280 --> 00:27:37,840
And that brings us past the Ottoman borders and into western Europe.

264
00:27:37,840 --> 00:27:43,440
Coffee might have arrived on European soil in the 1520s, but given the intense distrust between the

265
00:27:43,440 --> 00:27:48,320
Christian West and the Ottoman Turks, this would go unnoticed on the rest of the continent for a

266
00:27:48,320 --> 00:27:55,120
very long time. The first actual record of coffee in Western writing comes in 1582, when a German

267
00:27:55,120 --> 00:28:00,720
doctor tried it on a visit to Aleppo, describing it as a black beverage good for digestion.

268
00:28:00,720 --> 00:28:03,600
Nine years later coffee would be introduced to Venetian traders,

269
00:28:03,600 --> 00:28:09,120
who bought a few bags from Istanbul and pitched it as a medicine. But this would be a hard sell;

270
00:28:09,120 --> 00:28:14,640
after all, word began to spread that this was a Muslim delicacy, and that was problematic.

271
00:28:14,640 --> 00:28:20,480
Cardinals in the Vatican lobbied the Pope, Clement VIII, to ban coffee outright- to make it illegal

272
00:28:20,480 --> 00:28:25,280
for Christian consumers. But the Pope decided he should try it before making any judgements

273
00:28:25,280 --> 00:28:32,800
and in 1600, he famously had a taste, liked it and gave coffee the papal seal of approval.

274
00:28:32,800 --> 00:28:38,560
Just like Kaldi and the goats, this is a famous story. And it is true- but it didn’t actually

275
00:28:38,560 --> 00:28:43,680
matter, because the bigger problem wasn’t God, it was money. The Ottomans still held

276
00:28:43,680 --> 00:28:48,080
a monopoly from al-Mocha, and their prices were so high that coffee was out of reach

277
00:28:48,080 --> 00:28:52,960
to all but the wealthiest foreign customers. It would take another four decades after the

278
00:28:52,960 --> 00:28:57,600
Pope’s blessing for the first café to open in Western Europe, and that was only after

279
00:28:57,600 --> 00:29:03,360
the Ottomans were kicked out of Yemen in a 1638 uprising. Finally in control of their own country,

280
00:29:03,360 --> 00:29:08,000
the Yemeni people and the new Qasimid Dynasty set out to build a new economy

281
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:13,440
based on selling lots and lots of coffee, and re-opened trade links with the West.

282
00:29:13,440 --> 00:29:18,320
The first café in Christian Europe would open in Venice in 1645, and before long

283
00:29:18,320 --> 00:29:23,680
the floodgates would open; coffeeshops in Italy followed by more in England, Germany,

284
00:29:23,680 --> 00:29:29,360
France, the Netherlands and beyond. And the impact on life in Europe would be monumental.

285
00:29:29,360 --> 00:29:33,360
In Austria, the Viennese introduced the idea of filtering coffee, and serving it

286
00:29:33,360 --> 00:29:39,280
with sides like cream and sugar. The Austrians also pioneered the sale of coffeeshop pastries,

287
00:29:39,280 --> 00:29:44,800
debuting the Kipferl in the 1680s, which would eventually become the croissant.

288
00:29:44,800 --> 00:29:50,080
Soon enough bakers everywhere were adding their own specialties to local cafes; brioche with nuts

289
00:29:50,080 --> 00:29:54,960
and raisins first made in Germany and sold as coffeecake, and the cinnamon bun debuted

290
00:29:54,960 --> 00:29:59,680
in the coffeeshops of Stockholm, Sweden. Cafes would become the center of social

291
00:29:59,680 --> 00:30:04,160
life in France, cradles of music in what’s now Germany and the Netherlands but most of

292
00:30:04,160 --> 00:30:10,480
all when we are talking about 17th century coffeeshops, this is a story about London.

293
00:30:10,480 --> 00:30:14,800
It is very difficult to express how significant the café became to everyday

294
00:30:14,800 --> 00:30:20,720
life in England. As just a few examples- there was one coffeeshop owned by a guy named Edward Lloyd,

295
00:30:20,720 --> 00:30:25,520
where boat captains and merchants used to meet up and talk about conditions at sea.

296
00:30:25,520 --> 00:30:30,480
This would evolve into Lloyds of London, the legendary insurance marketplace. And speaking

297
00:30:30,480 --> 00:30:35,200
of markets, John Castaing used to post up at Jonathan’s Coffeeshop, where he would

298
00:30:35,200 --> 00:30:41,200
update patrons on the day’s commodities pricing, eventually growing into the London Stock Exchange.

299
00:30:41,200 --> 00:30:46,080
By the way, Jonathan’s Coffeeshop would also gain notoriety as the hangout for Voltaire after his

300
00:30:46,080 --> 00:30:51,760
expulsion from France. In that café he became friends with the niece of Isaac Newton, who told

301
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:56,400
him a story about her uncle and an apple falling from a tree. Voltaire scribbled this on a napkin

302
00:30:56,400 --> 00:31:01,440
and he would publish the tale to great fanfare. As for Isaac Newton himself, well his hangout

303
00:31:01,440 --> 00:31:06,640
was a different London Café, the Grecian, where he would famously stay up long hours drinking

304
00:31:06,640 --> 00:31:11,600
coffee with Edmund Halley and other academic titans, together formulating theories that

305
00:31:11,600 --> 00:31:18,000
would change our knowledge of math and science. We are by no means done. In the early 1700s,

306
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:22,400
customers in London Cafes began bringing in items for sale- paintings, rare books

307
00:31:22,400 --> 00:31:27,680
and imported pottery. A system would begin where someone would light a small candle and customers

308
00:31:27,680 --> 00:31:33,360
could make offers for whatever was for sale; when the candle burned out, the highest offer won. Not

309
00:31:33,360 --> 00:31:38,720
only would this create the modern auction, but the world’s most famous auction houses, Sotheby’s and

310
00:31:38,720 --> 00:31:44,800
Christies, both began inside London coffeeshops. And so did prominent magazines and newspapers-

311
00:31:44,800 --> 00:31:50,880
The Spectator and the Tatler as two examples, both not just created for café customers but actually,

312
00:31:50,880 --> 00:31:57,840
at least in the beginning, written in coffeeshops. Cafes in London served as post offices. When ships

313
00:31:57,840 --> 00:32:03,040
were about to sail overseas, captains would hang a bag on the wall of their favorite café with the

314
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:08,240
name of the destination, and anyone could drop off a letter or package for shipping for a small fee.

315
00:32:08,240 --> 00:32:11,680
And speaking of small fees, it was in these establishments where baristas

316
00:32:11,680 --> 00:32:16,880
first placed cups on the counter, labeled “T.I.P.S.”…at least according to legend,

317
00:32:16,880 --> 00:32:23,040
“To Insure Prompt Service.” The first tip jars. Anyway. With so much of English and European

318
00:32:23,040 --> 00:32:27,600
culture moving out of traditional spaces and into cafes, it wouldn’t take long

319
00:32:27,600 --> 00:32:36,400
before the pushback would begin. And that brings us to the next part of this story.

320
00:32:36,400 --> 00:32:41,440
In spite of all the advancements in coffee culture, the 1660s were not a great time to be

321
00:32:41,440 --> 00:32:47,840
in London. A massive Bubonic Plague epidemic swept through town in 1664 and ’65, and as soon as that

322
00:32:47,840 --> 00:32:53,440
was under control, the Great Fire of London pretty much leveled everything inside the city walls.

323
00:32:53,440 --> 00:32:58,480
Now none of this was the fault of the king, Charles II, but since it happened on his watch

324
00:32:58,480 --> 00:33:03,600
he began to worry about public perception. To placate the people he introduced radical

325
00:33:03,600 --> 00:33:08,480
reforms granting religious freedom. A nice idea. But instead of calming things down it would set

326
00:33:08,480 --> 00:33:14,240
off a furor where radicals in each group started blaming the king for supporting their enemies, and

327
00:33:14,240 --> 00:33:20,160
King Charles would descend into full-on paranoia. Terrified that everyone was plotting against him,

328
00:33:20,160 --> 00:33:27,040
in 1675 the king would issue an order to shut down all of England’s cafes, to put an end to

329
00:33:27,040 --> 00:33:33,680
socializing. This did not go over well. So intense would be the backlash that just eleven days later

330
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:39,600
he would back down and rescind the order, then live out his days in terror of enemies all around.

331
00:33:39,600 --> 00:33:45,360
King Charles II, and Murad IV in Turkey were not completely off base in assuming coffeeshops were

332
00:33:45,360 --> 00:33:51,360
centers of anti-government radicalism. They were- and not just because of all the people coming

333
00:33:51,360 --> 00:33:56,960
together to socialize. That had been happening since the beginning of time, but in the past the

334
00:33:56,960 --> 00:34:03,200
drink of choice was beer, and beer kept the masses drunk, unfocused, and unthreatening- at least

335
00:34:03,200 --> 00:34:08,719
to those in power. But coffee had the opposite effect and all of a sudden, people were thinking,

336
00:34:08,719 --> 00:34:14,719
and to an unelected leader, thinking is dangerous. As coffee spread across the old world there would

337
00:34:14,719 --> 00:34:19,040
be countless attempts at crackdowns and regulations, negative PR campaigns and

338
00:34:19,040 --> 00:34:24,960
full-on bans- see our story from the open about Johan Sebastian Bach. And while we need to move

339
00:34:24,960 --> 00:34:29,679
on here are two more insane examples. The first takes us to Prussia,

340
00:34:29,679 --> 00:34:34,639
where Frederick the Great was not a fan of coffee, and especially began to worry that it

341
00:34:34,639 --> 00:34:40,800
was infiltrating the peasant class, who he relied on for blind support of all of his foreign wars.

342
00:34:40,800 --> 00:34:46,239
So not only did he outlaw imported coffee, but he went as far as to hire 400 combat veterans to

343
00:34:46,239 --> 00:34:51,840
patrol the streets of Prussian cities, literally smelling the air to try to detect the scent of

344
00:34:51,840 --> 00:34:57,920
illicit coffee being roasted. These men would be known as Prussia’s Kaffeeschnuffler, or coffee

345
00:34:57,920 --> 00:35:03,520
sniffers, and that is still less ridiculous than the next story, which comes from Sweden.

346
00:35:03,520 --> 00:35:08,720
In this case, the anti-coffee-monarch was King Gustav III, who really wanted to ban

347
00:35:08,720 --> 00:35:14,000
coffee but worried about the public reaction. So he set out to prove that coffee was toxic,

348
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:19,280
so no one could argue when he outlawed cafes. As an experiment he found a pair of identical

349
00:35:19,280 --> 00:35:22,880
twins who had committed a murder and were both sentenced to death.

350
00:35:22,880 --> 00:35:27,360
The king offered to commute their sentences to life in prison, with the caveat that for the

351
00:35:27,360 --> 00:35:33,040
rest of their lives one must drink three pots of coffee a day, and the other could only drink tea.

352
00:35:33,040 --> 00:35:38,640
The idea was to prove that tea was safe and coffee was not. This would fail. Both brothers

353
00:35:38,640 --> 00:35:42,880
would outlive the king. They’d survive beyond any of the doctors who observed

354
00:35:42,880 --> 00:35:48,240
their condition and to this day Sweden is among the world’s leading coffee-drinking countries.

355
00:35:48,240 --> 00:35:53,840
Anyway I can’t absolutely say for sure that this happened. It feels like a legend. But

356
00:35:53,840 --> 00:35:58,880
honestly who even cares because the moral of the story is an absolute fact. Once coffee

357
00:35:58,880 --> 00:36:04,160
arrived anywhere- it was there to stay. So let’s take a break for a coffee

358
00:36:04,160 --> 00:36:24,320
and a croissant. 

359
00:36:24,320 --> 00:36:30,560
The modern coffeeshop- with its shelf of pastries, selection of coffee drinks and lively atmosphere-

360
00:36:30,560 --> 00:36:36,720
takes bits and pieces from much of the world. Mecca, Istanbul, Stockholm and London…but one

361
00:36:36,720 --> 00:36:42,080
could say the real point of origin when it all turned into this goes back to 1837,

362
00:36:42,080 --> 00:36:47,840
when a Viennese businessman named August Zang moved to Paris and opened a bakery. He’d introduce

363
00:36:47,840 --> 00:36:53,120
to France the Austrian technique of steam baking- basically injecting steam into the hot

364
00:36:53,120 --> 00:36:59,200
oven to keep dough soft and fluffy while the crust hardens. Allowing the innovation of the baguette,

365
00:36:59,200 --> 00:37:04,720
the croissant, and the entire category of French pastry. It wasn’t long before these

366
00:37:04,720 --> 00:37:09,840
would spread to Parisian cafes, along with the Austrian style of adding dense whipped cream

367
00:37:09,840 --> 00:37:15,520
to strong coffee- what the French would call Café Viennois. Then soon enough more coffee

368
00:37:15,520 --> 00:37:20,640
and dairy combinations would emerge intended to pair with pastries and the whole world’s morning

369
00:37:20,640 --> 00:37:44,720
culture would change. 

