00:39:44,800 --> 00:39:49,280
In the year 1616, thirty years before the first café would open in Venice,

383
00:39:49,280 --> 00:39:54,800
the Dutch sent a spy into Yemen to try to smuggle out some coffee and break the monopoly.

384
00:39:54,800 --> 00:39:59,280
The name of this sailor was Pieter van den Broecke. And if that name sounds familiar

385
00:39:59,280 --> 00:40:04,080
that is the exact same guy who would later set up the first nutmeg plantation on the

386
00:40:04,080 --> 00:40:09,760
Banda Islands. We literally interviewed his great-great-great-whatever grandson. And

387
00:40:09,760 --> 00:40:14,960
somehow we never realized his connection to coffee because it would be pretty much lost to history.

388
00:40:14,960 --> 00:40:18,800
Basically Mr. Van den Broecke would actually succeed and manage to sneak out

389
00:40:18,800 --> 00:40:22,880
a few raw coffee beans from al-Mocha. He’d bring them back to Amsterdam,

390
00:40:22,880 --> 00:40:28,160
but coffee doesn’t grow in the Dutch climate, so nothing would happen. At first.

391
00:40:28,160 --> 00:40:33,280
Now- I cannot express how wild this is- but somehow botanists at the Amsterdam

392
00:40:33,280 --> 00:40:38,720
Botanical Gardens for the next eight decades managed to keep these coffee plants alive in

393
00:40:38,720 --> 00:40:45,440
a small greenhouse. And then in 1696, half a century after Pieter van den Broecke had died,

394
00:40:45,440 --> 00:40:49,280
a few cuttings of these plants were brought along on a boat sailing halfway around the

395
00:40:49,280 --> 00:41:03,280
world to the Dutch colony of Java. And the coffee industry would change forever.

396
00:41:03,280 --> 00:41:09,520
In 1711 the first shipment of Dutch coffee, grown in Indonesia would reach the European market,

397
00:41:09,520 --> 00:41:15,280
and this would prove so successful, so in demand that within one single decade two-thirds of the

398
00:41:15,280 --> 00:41:22,560
global coffee trade was coming from Java. And after 260 years, the al-Mocha monopoly was over.

399
00:41:22,560 --> 00:41:27,440
This would lead to a lot of consequences. For one thing, the price of coffee plummeted,

400
00:41:27,440 --> 00:41:32,560
which led to the profusion of more cafes and the rise of home coffee consumption.

401
00:41:32,560 --> 00:41:38,160
The economy of Yemen collapsed; since the Dutch relied on slaves, their coffee was cheaper;

402
00:41:38,160 --> 00:41:43,280
Yemen could not compete, and the country fell into a depression. Within a generation most of

403
00:41:43,280 --> 00:41:48,560
the old coffee farms were gone; abandoned or replaced with other crops like Khat,

404
00:41:48,560 --> 00:41:52,480
a stimulant chewed for a quick buzz. Then there would be the fallout for

405
00:41:52,480 --> 00:41:56,640
the Javanese, the native people of the island. Since the Indonesian colony

406
00:41:56,640 --> 00:42:02,320
was governed not by Holland but by the Dutch East India Company, a for-profit enterprise,

407
00:42:02,320 --> 00:42:07,520
coffee became a key commodity- and that meant a lot of forced labor, exploitation, ecological

408
00:42:07,520 --> 00:42:14,240
disaster and full-on massacres, the worst carried out against the Sundanese of West Java; entire

409
00:42:14,240 --> 00:42:19,840
villages executed for coming in below quota. Just like everything about the Dutch East Indies,

410
00:42:19,840 --> 00:42:24,960
the coffee trade was awful. But there was one interesting legacy that came from the harsh

411
00:42:24,960 --> 00:42:30,080
treatment of the local people. The Indonesian farmers were banned from drinking coffee;

412
00:42:30,080 --> 00:42:35,520
every bean was money meant for export. But at some point somebody noticed that a native animal,

413
00:42:35,520 --> 00:42:41,760
the Asian palm civet, fed on coffee plants and seemed to eat only the best quality berries.

414
00:42:41,760 --> 00:42:46,320
Then the civet would digest the fruit but not the bean, which would pass through their system

415
00:42:46,320 --> 00:42:51,920
and be collected, the farmers keeping them for special occasions and creating what we now call

416
00:42:51,920 --> 00:42:57,600
Kopi Luwak, the world’s most expensive coffee. Anyway in time the Dutch would expand their coffee

417
00:42:57,600 --> 00:43:03,920
production to other parts of Indonesia; to Sumatra and Borneo and Sulawesi- but on that island they

418
00:43:03,920 --> 00:43:11,120
found something unexpected…coffee. Already there. And that is another fascinating story.

419
00:43:11,120 --> 00:43:16,160
Now I’m going to start by saying this is a legend. I have no idea if it’s true. There are

420
00:43:16,160 --> 00:43:21,280
a lot of different versions online and in old writing. But the gist of it is that sometime,

421
00:43:21,280 --> 00:43:28,320
probably in the 1500s, a Muslim from India named Baba Budan took a pilgrimage to Mecca. On his way

422
00:43:28,320 --> 00:43:32,960
home he traveled through Yemen, heading towards the port to catch his ship. He fell in love with

423
00:43:32,960 --> 00:43:38,400
coffee, and since he knew it did not exist in India, he stole exactly seven raw coffee beans

424
00:43:38,400 --> 00:43:43,760
and hid them in his beard, then planted them in the hills of Karnataka, his home province,

425
00:43:43,760 --> 00:43:49,200
and thus would begin the Indian coffee industry. The story goes that some south Indian sailor must

426
00:43:49,200 --> 00:43:54,480
have brought some coffee along on a trading voyage to Sulawesi, and that’s how the plant arrived.

427
00:43:54,480 --> 00:43:59,600
Again I have no idea if this story is true. But I don’t have a better explanation,

428
00:43:59,600 --> 00:44:04,160
and it’s also worth mentioning that in the 1840s British colonists deep in the

429
00:44:04,160 --> 00:44:11,680
hills of Karnataka found coffee plantations that apparently had been there for a very long time.

430
00:44:11,680 --> 00:44:12,960
Anyway. The Dutch

431
00:44:12,960 --> 00:44:18,240
dominance in the global coffee industry would be short-lived. And that was because of what might be

432
00:44:18,240 --> 00:44:34,480
the costliest present in global farming history. In 1714, as a token of friendship to celebrate the

433
00:44:34,480 --> 00:44:40,880
end of the War of Spanish Succession, the mayor of Amsterdam, a Mr. Nicolaas Witsen, presented

434
00:44:40,880 --> 00:44:47,520
one single coffee tree as a gift to Louis XIV, the King of France. The king was over the moon- this

435
00:44:47,520 --> 00:44:52,880
was among the rarest items in the Western world…so he showed it off to the public, and had a special

436
00:44:52,880 --> 00:44:58,080
greenhouse constructed just to keep it safe. Nine years later, across the Atlantic on the

437
00:44:58,080 --> 00:45:03,120
island of Martinique, a highborn French soldier named Gabriel de Clieu was about to head home

438
00:45:03,120 --> 00:45:08,000
at the end of his rotation. Somehow he heard a rumor that the Dutch were having some success

439
00:45:08,000 --> 00:45:14,240
planting coffee in the New World, so he came up with an idea. De Clieu hatched a plan to use a

440
00:45:14,240 --> 00:45:20,160
family connection at the palace to sneak into what was now Louis XV’s greenhouse and cut a

441
00:45:20,160 --> 00:45:25,360
single seedling from the royal plant. Then if all went well, he would smuggle it back to Martinique,

442
00:45:25,360 --> 00:45:30,240
keep it alive on the dangerous ocean crossing, somehow grow it into a massive plantation and

443
00:45:30,240 --> 00:45:36,480
become a national hero. In the end, that is exactly what would happen.

444
00:45:36,480 --> 00:45:40,240
So incredibly well would this plant take to Martinique that within a generation,

445
00:45:40,240 --> 00:45:45,360
Martinique and the rest of the French New World colonies supplied more than half of all the

446
00:45:45,360 --> 00:45:51,280
coffee sold across the entire global market. But just like for the Dutch this was not to

447
00:45:51,280 --> 00:45:57,360
last. In 1727, the Portuguese in Brazil decided they wanted a piece of the action. So they sent

448
00:45:57,360 --> 00:46:02,880
an envoy to French Guyana to negotiate the price for a plant. The Guyanese governor said

449
00:46:02,880 --> 00:46:08,400
absolutely not, our coffee is not for sale. But this is when I should mention that the envoy,

450
00:46:08,400 --> 00:46:15,280
a Mr. Francisco de Melo Palheta, was very handsome. Apparently legendarily handsome,

451
00:46:15,280 --> 00:46:20,160
and in his time in Cayenne he made quite an impression on the Governor’s wife. And when he

452
00:46:20,160 --> 00:46:25,600
left, she presented him with a bouquet of flowers with a few raw coffee beans hidden in the bottom.

453
00:46:25,600 --> 00:46:30,800
And that was how the Brazilian coffee industry would start- to this day still the biggest in

454
00:46:30,800 --> 00:46:35,040
the world. With the Portuguese and Dutch now in possession of coffee the French

455
00:46:35,040 --> 00:46:40,640
said  it and agreed to sell some to the English, in Jamaica, where in 1728 they’d

456
00:46:40,640 --> 00:46:44,800
plant what is now known as Blue Mountain. Within the next few years, coffee farming

457
00:46:44,800 --> 00:46:50,800
would expand to Cuba, Mexico, El Salvador, Costa Rica, pretty much all across the American tropics

458
00:46:50,800 --> 00:46:56,640
and all of it- literally almost all the coffee ever farmed in the entire western hemisphere-

459
00:46:56,640 --> 00:47:02,480
descended from that one single plant smuggled from Louis XV’s palace. A plant that itself descended

460
00:47:02,480 --> 00:47:08,080
from one single raw coffee bean stolen from Yemen by Pieter Van den Broecke. And that raw coffee

461
00:47:08,080 --> 00:47:14,960
bean belonged to a plant once taken across the Red Sea directly from the Oromo traders of Ethiopia.

462
00:47:14,960 --> 00:47:35,600
I think it’s time for a coffee. 

463
00:47:35,600 --> 00:47:40,240
It’s easy to follow the spread of European colonialism and see how coffee reached a

464
00:47:40,240 --> 00:47:45,120
huge part of the world- the tropics of the Americas, sub-Saharan Africa and here in

465
00:47:45,120 --> 00:47:49,920
Southeast Asia. Anywhere the climate was suitable, by the late 19th century coffee

466
00:47:49,920 --> 00:47:55,680
was probably growing. But that doesn’t mean it immediately became part of the native culture.

467
00:47:55,680 --> 00:48:00,320
In some cases that was by force. Like how the Dutch banned the Indonesian locals from

468
00:48:00,320 --> 00:48:06,480
drinking coffee, so too did the British pass laws against the Kenyans, with even one unauthorized

469
00:48:06,480 --> 00:48:12,400
coffee tree enough for a prison sentence. Never mind the fact that coffee in Kenya grows wild.

470
00:48:12,400 --> 00:48:17,120
Sometimes coffee wasn’t quote-illegal; it was just strictly segregated and kept far away

471
00:48:17,120 --> 00:48:24,000
from local people- French Indochina as a classic example, even as Vietnam’s coffee farms exploded.

472
00:48:24,000 --> 00:48:28,160
And then there were places like Hong Kong where coffee was around, it just didn’t suit

473
00:48:28,160 --> 00:48:33,120
the local palate, until new innovations started to change the native perception.

474
00:48:33,120 --> 00:48:36,720
The decades between the early 20th century and the end of the colonial

475
00:48:36,720 --> 00:48:41,920
era were kind of a third coffee “Big Bang”- local people from one side of the planet

476
00:48:41,920 --> 00:48:46,640
to the other adding their own flavors and techniques to coffee, and in the process,

477
00:48:46,640 --> 00:48:52,240
making something once forbidden completely their own. As just one example take the Malaysian and

478
00:48:52,240 --> 00:48:58,560
Singaporean Kopitiam. Beginning with the Hainanese domestic workers in British colonial households,

479
00:48:58,560 --> 00:49:05,520
introduced to the public in the 1920s, developing over time into a brand new category. Coffee beans

480
00:49:05,520 --> 00:49:10,640
roasted with sugar, butter and salt, cut with wheat or dried corn to make the mixture more

481
00:49:10,640 --> 00:49:17,200
affordable. Then add in some condensed milk or mix half-and-half with tea and you have the start of a

482
00:49:17,200 --> 00:49:43,760
perfect Malaysian morning. 

483
00:49:43,760 --> 00:49:50,080
Ok. We are reaching the point in the shoot when if I don’t keep drinking coffee at the same rate

484
00:49:50,080 --> 00:49:55,840
that we have since we started, I’m going to crash, and it’s all going to be over. So…I

485
00:49:55,840 --> 00:50:02,720
can barely pick it up! What are you drinking? I have Kopi- regular coffee I think? It’s coffee

486
00:50:02,720 --> 00:50:07,440
with milk. There is Kopi O, Kopi something…mine is just regular. With the condensed milk. So yours

487
00:50:07,440 --> 00:50:13,680
is the Malaysian proper kopitiam coffee. Mine is “cham”. It’s hard to drink this- it’s so frothy,

488
00:50:13,680 --> 00:50:21,120
the Malaysian way of making coffee- it’s so hard with a mustache. This is… This is Cham,

489
00:50:21,120 --> 00:50:28,080
it’s half coffee, half tea. A kopitiam staple. This is sort of the first wave of coffeeshops

490
00:50:28,080 --> 00:50:34,560
that were for the public in Southeast Asia. The beginning of the coffee story starts with the

491
00:50:34,560 --> 00:50:41,920
colonists. It took a very long time before it was accepted by or presented to the actual people who

492
00:50:41,920 --> 00:50:46,160
are from here. But it started in Singapore and Malaysia with the kopitiam culture,

493
00:50:46,160 --> 00:50:51,200
which is what would influence Thailand. We’ve done a whole lot on that on the channel before,

494
00:50:51,200 --> 00:50:56,720
I’ll link that in the top pinned comment. I’m consciously slowing myself down as I

495
00:50:56,720 --> 00:51:06,400
talk. I have this- if I could start…I love living in Bangkok, I love our life where it

496
00:51:06,400 --> 00:51:13,280
is right now. But Malaysian mornings, to start in a kopitiam- to me, that’s

497
00:51:13,280 --> 00:51:26,640
the best possible start to a day. I’m just like …I’m in between my coffee kicks now.

498
00:51:26,640 --> 00:51:33,440
So I find this almost subversive. Like, when the whole coffee culture started in Southeast Asia,

499
00:51:33,440 --> 00:51:41,600
not for colonists but for the actual people- it kind of almost started as rebellion. Like…in

500
00:51:41,600 --> 00:51:48,240
Malaysia- we’re at a kopitiam right now- Malaysia and Singapore, that was one side of the story,

501
00:51:48,240 --> 00:51:52,240
obviously which we’ve covered here on the channel before, but the domestic workers in

502
00:51:52,240 --> 00:51:58,000
the English households, who learned the recipes for coffee, toast- this is with the “kaya”, which

503
00:51:58,000 --> 00:52:03,680
is a coconut custard that was first influenced by the Portuguese when they were the colonists. It’s

504
00:52:03,680 --> 00:52:10,720
such an interesting story…we see the same thing in Hong Kong, where the chachaanteng culture,

505
00:52:10,720 --> 00:52:15,680
which translates to “tea house,” but it’s very coffee-focused, and chachaanteng culture in

506
00:52:15,680 --> 00:52:20,480
Hong Kong also started with “how do we take these dishes that we’re almost prohibited from eating,

507
00:52:20,480 --> 00:52:25,040
or drinks that we’re prohibited from having, and present them in a way that’s affordable to the

508
00:52:25,040 --> 00:52:34,880
masses.” Macaroni with ketchup. Deep-fried peanut butter French toast. And then obviously Vietnam is

509
00:52:34,880 --> 00:52:44,160
the classic example, because how everything was adapted for local people in rebellion against

510
00:52:44,160 --> 00:52:49,520
the French banning them from having coffee. It’s such an interesting chapter in history,

511
00:52:49,520 --> 00:52:56,320
and don’t underestimate the coffee culture in Southeast Asia. It’s absolutely phenomenal,

512
00:52:56,320 --> 00:52:59,360
and I’m just going to keep rambling, because I’ve had a bunch of coffee and

513
00:52:59,360 --> 00:53:41,920
I can’t stop myself. 

514
00:53:41,920 --> 00:53:45,840
If you’ve watched these videos before you probably know what’s coming next.

515
00:53:45,840 --> 00:53:50,240
Monoculture farming- in this case an extreme example, pretty much always ends the same

516
00:53:50,240 --> 00:53:54,960
way- with some awful pest or disease that rips through the global harvest.

517
00:53:54,960 --> 00:54:00,480
In the case of coffee it was a fungus, first identified in 1869 in Sri Lanka and quickly

518
00:54:00,480 --> 00:54:04,880
devastating the entire local industry. Then from there it would spread to India,

519
00:54:04,880 --> 00:54:09,280
then Southeast Asia, and soon the entire Eastern Hemisphere was in trouble.

520
00:54:09,280 --> 00:54:14,080
Now at first there was nothing anyone could do. Plantation owners switched to growing tea,

521
00:54:14,080 --> 00:54:20,560
and all across Europe tea consumption skyrocketed; coffeeshops rebranding as teahouses. For the last

522
00:54:20,560 --> 00:54:24,800
two decades of the 19th century coffee became essentially a New World drink,

523
00:54:24,800 --> 00:54:30,480
grown and consumed pretty much only in the west, where the blight thankfully did not take hold.

524
00:54:30,480 --> 00:54:35,200
But then in 1895 a French botanist realized that the people in Central Africa- what’s

525
00:54:35,200 --> 00:54:40,080
now Gabon and the Democratic Republic of the Congo- were growing and consuming a native

526
00:54:40,080 --> 00:54:44,480
strain of coffee that was not the same as the one the rest of the world knew.

527
00:54:44,480 --> 00:54:49,520
It turned out this varietal grew in lowlands, tolerating high heat- perfect for the Asian

528
00:54:49,520 --> 00:54:54,160
tropics. And even better, it was almost completely resistant to the devastating

529
00:54:54,160 --> 00:54:59,200
coffee fungus. At first this new strain would be given the name Coffea canephora,

530
00:54:59,200 --> 00:55:04,640
but in 1900 at the Paris World’s Fair it would be introduced to the world as “Robusta,” and

531
00:55:04,640 --> 00:55:10,800
the old varietal, what had been called Mocha or simply “coffee”, came to be known as “Arabica.”

532
00:55:10,800 --> 00:55:15,040
Now- robusta coffee is very different from Arabica. It’s more bitter, has a stronger

533
00:55:15,040 --> 00:55:20,240
flavor and pretty soon, scientists realized it could hold up to freeze-drying or preserving

534
00:55:20,240 --> 00:55:26,080
in ways Arabica could not. And so the instant coffee revolution would kick into high gear.

535
00:55:26,080 --> 00:55:32,240
The idea for making a fast packet-coffee started as far back as 1881, with a French soldier named

536
00:55:32,240 --> 00:55:37,840
Alphonse Allais who found himself frustrated with the coffee rations on the battlefield, so he

537
00:55:37,840 --> 00:55:43,200
developed a method of dehydrating brewed coffee. But his invention was grainy and hard to drink,

538
00:55:43,200 --> 00:55:49,920
so enter David Strang of New Zealand, who came up with a water-soluble coffee powder in 1890.

539
00:55:49,920 --> 00:55:55,680
A few years later this would be perfected by Satori Kato, from Japan working in Chicago, who

540
00:55:55,680 --> 00:56:01,200
made that powder shelf-stable and long-lasting. And finally after the introduction of Robusta,

541
00:56:01,200 --> 00:56:06,640
this would be turned into a commercial product by George Washington- a different George Washington,

542
00:56:06,640 --> 00:56:11,360
this one born in Belgium, working in Brooklyn. Washington’s coffee would be bought out by the

543
00:56:11,360 --> 00:56:16,480
United States Army during World War I where it was given the slang name “George,” as in,

544
00:56:16,480 --> 00:56:22,800
a cup of George. How that eventually turned into a cup of Joe has been entirely lost to history.

545
00:56:22,800 --> 00:56:27,520
Anyway all these early versions of instant coffee still had to be brewed- boiled and filtered- and

546
00:56:27,520 --> 00:56:33,200
they weren’t really “instant.” Until in 1938 a Swiss chemist named Max Morgenthaler pioneered

547
00:56:33,200 --> 00:56:39,040
a truly revolutionary version- blending coffee extract with added carbohydrates to make something

548
00:56:39,040 --> 00:56:45,200
fast, efficient and packed with flavor. This would be introduced by Nestle as “Nescafe”.

549
00:56:45,200 --> 00:56:49,520
Now at the same time as instant coffee was coming into existence, a parallel movement

550
00:56:49,520 --> 00:56:55,280
was focused on increasing speed and quality at the same time. This would be centered in Italy,

551
00:56:55,280 --> 00:57:02,000
starting in 1884 when Angelo Moriondo of Torino built the first-ever steam-powered coffee machine.

552
00:57:02,000 --> 00:57:07,920
In 1901 this would be advanced by Luigi Bezzera of Milan, who refined the concept so it could

553
00:57:07,920 --> 00:57:13,280
brew single portions, one-by-one. Bezzera’s patent would be sold to an engineer named

554
00:57:13,280 --> 00:57:18,880
Desiderio Pavoni, who added some upgrades and then in 1906 introduced the machine and

555
00:57:18,880 --> 00:57:24,640
the concept of Espresso to the world. Believe it or not it wasn’t until 1947

556
00:57:24,640 --> 00:57:30,000
when espresso machines would be refined enough to start adding milk or cream into the brew, opening

557
00:57:30,000 --> 00:57:36,480
the door to stuff like cappuccino, café Latte and pretty much the entire modern category of coffee.

558
00:57:36,480 --> 00:57:41,200
As for the Americano, by the way, that also shows up around the same time in Italy- named for the

559
00:57:41,200 --> 00:57:46,560
American soldiers stationed after World War II who wanted their espresso watered down to taste

560
00:57:46,560 --> 00:57:51,840
more like the drip coffee they favored back home. Though it should be mentioned that drip coffee is

561
00:57:51,840 --> 00:57:57,840
not an American invention- that credit goes to a humble German housewife named Melitta Bentz,

562
00:57:57,840 --> 00:58:02,480
who in 1908 punched some holes in the bottom of a brass pot, lined it with a

563
00:58:02,480 --> 00:58:07,680
sheet of paper from her son’s school notebook and found the result to be very satisfying.

564
00:58:07,680 --> 00:58:11,920
Mrs. Bentz, by the way, would patent this invention, set up a company to

565
00:58:11,920 --> 00:58:16,560
mass-produce her creation and to this day Melitta is one of the world’s largest

566
00:58:16,560 --> 00:58:23,200
manufacturers of coffee-related accessories. Anyway. One last note before we move on.

567
00:58:23,200 --> 00:58:28,240
In case all this time you’ve been wondering why “mocha” today means coffee mixed with chocolate,

568
00:58:28,240 --> 00:58:33,760
that starts in the United States in the early 20th century with a craze for coffee-flavored sweets,

569
00:58:33,760 --> 00:58:39,360
especially cake frosting. This became known as Mocha cake, and to give it a bit more sweetness

570
00:58:39,360 --> 00:58:44,080
it became common for chocolate to be added into the recipe. The combination of coffee

571
00:58:44,080 --> 00:58:49,360
and chocolate would catch on and soon enough was applied to a drink, and that is where the

572
00:58:49,360 --> 00:59:07,505
new definition of Mocha came from. Let’s take one last coffee break.

573
00:59:07,505 --> 00:59:08,960
 A few last odds and ends

574
00:59:08,960 --> 00:59:14,320
before we move on. The coffee styles we see most often all around the world might have taken their

575
00:59:14,320 --> 00:59:20,640
current form after World War II. But the seeds at least in some cases were planted much earlier.

576
00:59:20,640 --> 00:59:26,400
The Cappuccino first shows up at least in name in 18th century Austria, the combination of milk

577
00:59:26,400 --> 00:59:33,280
and coffee said to resemble the brown robes of Capuchin monks. Hence the original term Kapuziner.

578
00:59:33,280 --> 00:59:38,880
Contrary to popular belief it is not Italian. That’s because around the same time in Italy the

579
00:59:38,880 --> 00:59:44,640
identical recipe took on its own name…Café Latte. There are some coffee styles that don’t show up

580
00:59:44,640 --> 00:59:51,440
until decades after the espresso revolution. The Cortado, for example, comes from 1960s Spain,

581
00:59:51,440 --> 00:59:57,520
with workers asking for their espresso to be cut- or cortar- with a bit of milk.

582
00:59:57,520 --> 01:00:02,640
The Long Black first appears in the 1950s, and the Flat White in the mid ‘80s. Both

583
01:00:02,640 --> 01:00:07,120
the subject of fierce argument between Australia and New Zealand- heated enough

