631
01:05:34,480 --> 01:05:39,040
Despite what you might assume, the first coffee chains were not American. Depending

632
01:05:39,040 --> 01:05:44,640
on what sources you read the idea of multi-outlet coffeeshops dates back to the early 20th century

633
01:05:44,640 --> 01:05:50,240
in England, or France, or even Japan, and the modern-everything-identical concept was

634
01:05:50,240 --> 01:05:56,320
actually pioneered in Austria, where by the 1920s Aida had a half-dozen locations all serving the

635
01:05:56,320 --> 01:06:02,000
same pastries and drinks in the same style. The first major American café brand was the

636
01:06:02,000 --> 01:06:07,360
memorably named Chock Full O’ Nuts- as you might expect originally a nut shop.

637
01:06:07,360 --> 01:06:12,000
And while chain stores in general do not have the best reputations, in this case,

638
01:06:12,000 --> 01:06:16,720
they were actually a pretty good company. Way before this was normal, they insisted on equal

639
01:06:16,720 --> 01:06:22,640
opportunity hiring- and refused to expand into the South because of laws enforcing segregation. They

640
01:06:22,640 --> 01:06:28,480
paid a living wage and provided all staff with health care and in 1957, hired the civil rights

641
01:06:28,480 --> 01:06:34,240
icon Jackie Robinson to serve as corporate Vice President when his baseball playing career ended.

642
01:06:34,240 --> 01:06:38,480
But that is just a side note because this is obviously about Starbucks.

643
01:06:38,480 --> 01:06:44,000
The biggest coffee empire in world history started in 1971 in Seattle, founded by three friends

644
01:06:44,000 --> 01:06:49,120
and based on a small chain called Peet’s, which they’d loved as students at the University of San

645
01:06:49,120 --> 01:06:54,400
Francisco. They’d name it Starbucks, not because of Moby Dick but because they liked the way the

646
01:06:54,400 --> 01:07:00,320
word sounded. Over the next thirteen years they’d grow to four or five Seattle locations, then sell

647
01:07:00,320 --> 01:07:05,600
their shares in Starbucks to outside investors. Now this sounds like the classic tragic story

648
01:07:05,600 --> 01:07:11,120
of people founding a big company and selling out too early. But that’s actually not what happened.

649
01:07:11,120 --> 01:07:16,880
This is one of the only examples I can think of where somehow everybody actually won. The first

650
01:07:16,880 --> 01:07:22,320
of the Starbucks co-founders, Jerry Baldwin- well he’d use his share of the money to buy out Peet’s,

651
01:07:22,320 --> 01:07:27,360
the brand’s original inspiration, and would grow that business large enough to take it public in

652
01:07:27,360 --> 01:07:32,960
2001. He’s now a Napa Valley winemaker and worth about half a billion dollars.

653
01:07:32,960 --> 01:07:38,640
The second partner, Gordon Bowker, well he’d scale up his side project, Redhook Brewery,

654
01:07:38,640 --> 01:07:44,400
which would also go public after merging with or acquiring a few other pioneering beer companies.

655
01:07:44,400 --> 01:07:49,920
He passed away in 2025 at the age of 82. And finally there was Zev Seigl, who actually

656
01:07:49,920 --> 01:07:55,360
left Starbucks before the sale and set out on a path of serial entrepreneurship, becoming a

657
01:07:55,360 --> 01:08:01,520
well-known business mentor and public speaker. As for Starbucks itself, it would also do pretty

658
01:08:01,520 --> 01:08:05,760
well. Bought out by Howard Schultz, who would lead a push for fast expansion,

659
01:08:05,760 --> 01:08:10,960
then listed as an IPO in 1992. Today there are more than 41,000

660
01:08:10,960 --> 01:08:15,440
Starbucks stores across the world, and you don’t need me to talk much more about Starbucks because

661
01:08:15,440 --> 01:08:20,640
you know what it is, you’ve seen it everywhere, and it is basically the McDonald’s of coffee.

662
01:08:20,640 --> 01:08:25,359
Or actually McDonalds is the McDonalds of coffee, because they are 2nd in the world in

663
01:08:25,359 --> 01:08:31,520
sales- pouring around three billion cups per year in their own worldwide venues. For the record just

664
01:08:31,520 --> 01:08:36,560
in case you’re wondering, the rest of the top 5 are China’s Luckin Coffee, Dunkin’ (formerly

665
01:08:36,560 --> 01:08:41,760
Dunkin Donuts), and Canada’s Tim Horton’s. There are positives about these massive

666
01:08:41,760 --> 01:08:47,040
coffee conglomerates- I’m going to be honest, I love Dunkin’ coffee. But the biggest positive

667
01:08:47,040 --> 01:08:52,800
is probably the response; the pushback against the corporatization of the category. According

668
01:08:52,800 --> 01:08:58,800
to most analysis, the so-called Third Wave started in 1995 with Chicago’s Intelligentsia

669
01:08:58,800 --> 01:09:03,920
Coffee and Durham North Carolina’s Counter Culture Coffee, then soon enough they would be followed by

670
01:09:03,920 --> 01:09:10,160
other artisanal roasters all across the planet- focusing not on sales volume but on supply chain,

671
01:09:10,160 --> 01:09:16,000
flavor, technique and quality and bringing us into an unequivocal coffee golden age.

672
01:09:16,000 --> 01:09:21,040
But that Golden Age might not last much longer. Every year as the climate gets warmer there

673
01:09:21,040 --> 01:09:25,680
is less arable land suitable for coffee farming. This is not alarmism.

674
01:09:25,680 --> 01:09:29,920
This is what is happening right now. In the coffee-growing part of the world,

675
01:09:29,920 --> 01:09:34,880
lowland plantations are already dying out. And to keep up with the suitable temperature range,

676
01:09:34,880 --> 01:09:39,279
farms are moving as much as 500 meters higher in elevation, which means more

677
01:09:39,279 --> 01:09:44,240
land clearing, which reinforces the cycle. At this moment the current prediction from

678
01:09:44,240 --> 01:09:50,960
trade organizations is that by 2050, global coffee production will be reduced by half versus where

679
01:09:50,960 --> 01:09:57,600
it is now, and that is best case scenario. The worst case is apocalyptic. Because of the effect

680
01:09:57,600 --> 01:10:02,880
of climate change on Central and East Africa, the vast majority of wild coffee strains are

681
01:10:02,880 --> 01:10:07,440
either already gone, or in imminent danger of extinction. And that means if there is another

682
01:10:07,440 --> 01:10:12,640
blight, fungus or disease, we are running out of options to find the next Robusta;

683
01:10:12,640 --> 01:10:17,360
the next bean that might save the day. This is not a political issue. This is

684
01:10:17,360 --> 01:10:21,760
not something up for debate, because it’s not theoretical. It is already happening,

685
01:10:21,760 --> 01:10:25,440
and all around the world coffee producers are sounding the alarm.

686
01:10:25,440 --> 01:10:30,960
Now this doesn’t mean that coffee is just all of a sudden going to disappear tomorrow. But it 1000%

687
01:10:30,960 --> 01:10:36,960
does mean we shouldn’t take coffee for granted. This incredible plant that rewrote history,

688
01:10:36,960 --> 01:10:42,320
that sparked so much creativity and innovation, the center of our social and business lives and

689
01:10:42,320 --> 01:10:47,360
something that just makes the world so much more interesting. From the mountains of Ethiopia to the

690
01:10:47,360 --> 01:10:53,680
Arabian Peninsula. The cafes of Istanbul to the coffeeshops of London. From Java to Martinique,

691
01:10:53,680 --> 01:10:58,800
Brazil to Vietnam to wherever you are- coffee deserves its due.

